Changelog

Unreleased

- Device scan passes only character and block devices: a FIFO under
  /dev used to reach the cgroup program and fail compilation for
  every container on that host. Pinned by test.

- Container layers verify before extract: digests check on download
  and each tar entry screens (absolute paths, escapes, symlinks,
  devices, size caps) before write. Pinned by test.
- Registry pulls stream with single-pass verification: bytes hash
  while writing, so a large blob never sits in daemon memory, and
  tag overwrites stay visible instead of silently winning. Pinned
  by test.
- Blob sync resumes mid-blob: per-peer cursors persist in bbolt and
  only advance, raw fetches obey the 32MB cap, and tag state stays
  coherent across ticks. Pinned by test.
- Discovery skips bad members with a warning instead of stalling:
  unparsable gossip entries and cert or DNS misconfigurations fail
  loudly at the edge and never break the join loop. Pinned by test.
- Transport dials honor context and sync validates strictly:
  cancelled dials abort the handshake, and oversized or malformed
  sync bodies reject before apply. Pinned by test.
- Runtime starts in dependency order and stops leaking: networking
  and stores come up before the reconciler, and bridge, NAT, and
  sampler goroutines release on shutdown. Pinned by test.
- Submits validate before commit: unknown fields, bad restart
  policies, negative memory, bad probes and ports, bad file
  pointers, self-dependencies, and cycles all fail with 400, and
  invalid synced lines skip instead of breaking replay. Torn
  journal tails (crash between write and newline) drop at most one
  line and rebuild the committed prefix. Pinned by test.
- Key files fail closed: a corrupt WireGuard key is kept on disk and
  rejected instead of silently regenerated, and world-readable Noise
  or WireGuard keys are refused at load. Pinned by test.

v1.1.1 (2026-10-16)

Containers now start end to end. A nodev bundle directory used to
kill every container in init, and fixing that promoted three latent
start bugs in init order; all four are fixed and recorded. The full
story lives in docs/nodev.

Container start

- Diagnose the /dev/null denial: bundles on a nodev mount fail
  every device open with a line identical to a rule bug. New
  docs/nodev with the four-gate model, the errno discriminator,
  the elimination runbook, and placement rules.
- Commandless submits run the image default: empty spec command falls
  back to image entrypoint plus cmd instead of panicking init on an
  empty argv. Both empty is a logged build error. Contract in
  docs/cli.
- Failed starts retry: pull removes the stale rootfs instead of
  wedging on already exists, and a failed start destroys its created
  container so the next tick recreates.
- Veth names fit the kernel: veth- plus the first 8 ID chars plus
  the end letter (14 chars), inside the 15-char IFNAMSIZ limit.
  Full UUIDs failed LinkAdd with ERANGE on any machine.

Devices and runtime

- Register the eBPF device backend, so non-empty rule sets apply on
  both hierarchies.
- Scanned devices allow explicitly instead of defaulting to deny;
  runc default devices ride ahead of scanned sensors; every bundle
  carries a working null pair (covered by test).
- Explicit per-workload cgroup scope in bundles.
- Initial starts take the init path, with the libcontainer re-exec
  init dispatched in main.
- Debug logging for device rule summaries and every destroy
  initiation, behind a validated --log-level flag (debug, info,
  warn, error).

Trail and docs

- RecordPosition releases the sampler lock before the track fsync,
  so one slow disk never stalls pressure sampling or reads.
- Trail page corrected: real lock scope, seed-then-publish boot
  ordering.
- Nodev page framed as a recorded incident, including the three
  failures the fix promoted.
